# TWO ARMS THAT TURN TOGETHER (M31).
#
# A relation couples two freedoms a mate solve would otherwise choose
# independently (roadmap §20.5). This one is a GEAR at 2:1 -- turn the first
# arm and the second turns twice as far, without anybody driving it.
#
# What makes this a real check rather than a picture: the second arm is never
# driven. Its angle is not typed anywhere. If the relation were stored and not
# applied -- which is the failure this whole milestone exists to avoid -- the
# script would still run, still save, and the second arm would simply sit at
# zero. So what is measured below is WHERE IT ENDS UP.
#
# Run it:
#   ep3d --script examples/gear-train.ep3ds

# --- the parts ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A plate with two pivots on it, and an arm. One part file, two bodies -- the
# same shape assembly-three-parts.ep3ds uses.

sketch Plate
tool rect
click -70 -25
click 70 25
pad Plate 8

# THE TWO PIVOTS, on the PART (roadmap §21). A connector is defined once here
# and every instance of this plate in every assembly has it -- it is not an
# assembly concept that happens to live on a part.
#
# The three numbers after the position are where the connector's +Z POINTS, and
# that is the whole act of saying where the hinge is: a revolute turns about
# +Z.
connector Left -40 0 8 0 0 1
connector Right 40 0 8 0 0 1

part

sketch Bar
tool rect
click 0 -5
click 60 5
pad Arm 6

# OFF THE ARM'S OWN ORIGIN, at the end. A connector at the centre of mass would
# make turning the arm move nothing measurable, and every check below would
# pass on an assembly that never moved.
connector Eye 0 0 0 0 0 1

save build/gear-parts.ep3d

# --- the assembly -------------------------------------------------------------

assembly GearTrain
insert Frame build/gear-parts.ep3d Plate
insert First build/gear-parts.ep3d Arm
insert Second build/gear-parts.ep3d Arm

# SOMETHING HAS TO BE FIXED, or a chain of mates is relative to nothing.
ground Frame

mate revolute Hinge1 Frame/Left First/Eye 0
mate revolute Hinge2 Frame/Right Second/Eye 0

# --- the relation -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# TWO MATES, and the ratio is turns per turn. Which FREEDOM on each mate is not
# typed here: a gear couples two rotations, so it takes each mate's first free
# rotation -- one rule, in FirstFreeComponentOfKind, shared by this script, the
# Assembly menu and the loader, so a click and a script cannot produce
# different gears.
#
# `reversed` would be the fourth word, and it is what two meshing gears
# actually do -- left out here so the arithmetic below stays readable.
relation gear Reduction Hinge1 Hinge2 2

echo --- both arms at 0 ---
solve
measure

# --- and now turn ONE of them -------------------------------------------------
#
# One number changes, and TWO arms move.
#
# The arm is 60 x 10 x 6 with its Eye at one end, so its centre of mass sits
# 30 mm out along itself. At 0 degrees that is +30 in x from its pivot; at
# 45 it is (21.213, 21.213); at 90 it is +30 in y.
#
# So driving Hinge1 to 45 degrees puts:
#   First   at 45 -- its centre at (-40 + 21.213, 21.213) = (-18.787, 21.213)
#   Second  at 90 -- its centre at ( 40 +  0.000, 30.000) = ( 40.000, 30.000)
#
# Second is at NINETY because of the relation and nothing else. Nobody typed
# it, and no mate constrains it to any particular angle.

drive Hinge1 45
echo --- Hinge1 at 45, so Hinge2 is at 90 ---
solve
measure

# Half again, and the far end follows: 22.5 in gives 45 out.
drive Hinge1 22.5
echo --- Hinge1 at 22.5, so Hinge2 is at 45 ---
solve
measure

save build/gear-train.ep3da

# The relation is IN that file (schema v34) -- open it again and the second arm
# is still geared to the first. A coupling that lived only in this session
# would be a feature that forgets itself.
